<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbeville</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abbeville is a town in the Somme <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somme-river">River Somme</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the capital of the County of Ponthieu and a prosperous cloth town, with the Gothic church of Saint-Vulfran. Prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> found nearby by Jacques Boucher de Perthes in the 19th century gave their name to the Abbevillian culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbeville.jpg" alt="Abbeville" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Emmanuelhammel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mus%C3%A9e_Boucher-de-Perthes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Abbeville lies on the river Somme in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, 20 km from the river's modern mouth in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>, about 45 km northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> and roughly 200 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Most of the town stands on the east bank and on an island, at the head of the Abbeville Canal. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was the lowest crossing point on the Somme, and Edward III's army crossed nearby shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> in 1346. It is the historical capital of the County of Ponthieu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The subsoil holds many remains of the Pleistocene, and their discovery helped found prehistory as a science. Jacques Boucher de Perthes found handaxes near Abbeville from 1838 and in 1846 published the first description pointing out that the stones had been chipped deliberately by early humans to form a tool. They were known as Abbevillian handaxes, though that term is becoming obsolete. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> occupied the place and named it Abbatis Villa, and the modern name means villa of the abbé, as it once depended on the Abbey of Saint-Riquier. Abbeville prided itself on never having been taken, was called Abbeville la pucelle, and received many privileges from the Capetian kings in reward for its loyalty.</p><p>In 1665 the Van Robais family founded the Manufacture Royale des Rames, making linens and tablecloths. The cloth trade declined after the revocation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a> and the departure of Protestants, and the closing of the river port because of silt also hurt the economy. In the 18th century, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>'s account, the judges of Abbeville condemned the Chevalier de La Barre to torture and a slow death for impious songs and for passing a procession without removing his hat. The German army bombed the town almost to rubble in one night in the early days of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Abbeville is a commune in the Somme department of the Hauts-de-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> region and a sub-prefecture of the department. Much of the town is modern and rebuilt. The Collegiate Church of Saint-Vulfran, begun in 1488 and continued in the 16th and 17th centuries, has a facade that is a masterpiece of flamboyant Gothic architecture, flanked by two towers, and was classified as a historical monument in 1840. The Boucher de Perthes Museum shows some of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, and the municipal library keeps 972 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, among them a Carolingian Gospel book dated 790–800.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abbeville” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ponthieu emerged around 980 when Hugh, a castellan of <b>Abbeville</b>, married a daughter of Hugh Capet and was recognised as count.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ponthieu">County of Ponthieu</a></li><li><blockquote>The Capuchins Claude d'Abbeville and Yves d'Évreux wrote detailed accounts of the Tupinambá, and <b>Abbeville</b> took several Tupinambá to Paris, where they were baptised before the court in 1613.</blockquote><a href="/state/equinoctial-france">Equinoctial France</a></li></ul></section>
